{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.689,
            0.048,
            0.86,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Fractions in Disguise"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.101,
            0.795,
            0.152
          ],
          "content": "More generally, y% of some value, say 80, is given by $\\frac{y}{100} \\times 80.$ We can also say that 45% of some value, say z, is given by ${ \\frac { 4 5 } { 1 0 0 } } \\times z .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.178,
            0.456,
            0.201
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Free-hand Computations"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.208,
            0.787,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "We just calculated 25% of 120. Is 25% the same as $\\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ <sup>th</sup> (a quarter)? Suppose we want to find 25% of 40. Is it the same as $\\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ <sup>th</sup> of 40? Yes, since 25 is $\\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ th (a quarter) of 100 $\\left( { \\frac { 2 5 } { 1 0 0 } } = { \\frac { 1 } { 4 } } \\right)$ Therefore 25% of $40=\\frac{25}{100}\\times40$ is the same as ${ \\frac { 1 } { 4 } } \\times 4 0 .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.316,
            0.846,
            0.357
          ],
          "content": "？ Try to calculate (without using pen and paper) the indicated percentages of the values shown in the table below. Write your answers in the table."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.371,
            0.789,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": "|  | 100 | 200 | 50 | 80 | 10 | 35 | 287 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 25% | 25 |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| 10% |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| 20% |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| 5% |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_table_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.632,
            0.799,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": "? How did you find these values? Discuss the methods with the class. Do you find anything interesting in the table?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.675,
            0.802,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": "You may have noticed that 20% of a value is double that of 10% of the same value. This will always happen as 20% (20 parts out of 100) is twice that of 10% (10 parts out of 100)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.643,
            0.902,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.741,
            0.845,
            0.781
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Using this understanding, mentally calculate how much 40% of the values in the table above would be."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.784,
            0.841,
            0.804
          ],
          "content": "What relationship do you observe among 20%, 5% and 25% of a value?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.802,
            0.845,
            0.841
          ],
          "content": "It appears that (20% of y) + (5% of y) = 25% of y. We can verify that this property always holds:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.355,
            0.839,
            0.642,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": "{ \\Big ( } { \\frac { 2 0 } { 1 0 0 } } \\times y { \\Big ) } + { \\Big ( } { \\frac { 5 } { 1 0 0 } } \\times y { \\Big ) } = { \\Big ( } { \\frac { 2 5 } { 1 0 0 } } \\times y { \\Big ) } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.882,
            0.913,
            0.903,
            0.935
          ],
          "content": "7"
        }
      ]
    }
